    "faq_XGjUai": {
      "type": "faq",
      "blocks": {
        "q_frozen": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Is it fresh or frozen?",
            "text": "<p>Frozen, on purpose. Everything is blast-frozen to &minus;10&deg;F at harvest and vacuum sealed, which holds texture and flavour far better than fish that has spent days in transit on ice. It is why a portion out of your freezer in November tastes like the day it was landed.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_arrive": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How does it arrive?",
            "text": "<p>Insulated and frozen, with a frozen delivery guarantee &mdash; the published terms are on our <a href=\"/policies/shipping-policy\">shipping policy</a> page. Move everything to the freezer when it lands; there is no need to be home at the moment of delivery.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_store": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How long can I keep it in the freezer?",
            "text": "<p>Each vacuum-sealed portion carries its own best-by date on the pack &mdash; go by that rather than a rule of thumb. Keep it sealed and at a steady freezer temperature, and do not refreeze anything you have already thawed.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_thaw": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Do I need to thaw it first?",
            "text": "<p>Yes, for all four of these recipes. Overnight in the refrigerator in the sealed pack is best &mdash; about 12 hours &mdash; then pat it dry. In a hurry, submerge the sealed pack in a bowl of chilled water for 20 to 30 minutes, changing the water once. Never at room temperature, and never in warm water.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_whole": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Is cooking a whole trout actually difficult?",
            "text": "<p>No, and it is the recipe we would point a nervous cook at first. It is a single sheet pan with nothing to flip and no pan to stick to. The only new skill is getting it off the bone at the table, which is three movements and is written out step by step in <a href=\"/blogs/seafood-recipes/whole-roasted-trout-blistered-grapes-potatoes\">the recipe</a>.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_feeds": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How many people does each item feed?",
            "text": "<p>It is on every product page and on the recipe cards above. As a guide for this menu: the whole trout and the steelhead loin each serve two, a 16 oz bag of shrimp serves four, and branzino comes as two 6 oz fillets.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_testing": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "What does the testing actually cover?",
            "text": "<p>Every supplier is tested before we approve them, then randomly audited to ensure consistent compliance with the Seatopia Standard &mdash; third-party lab tested for nutrition, mercury and microplastics. <a href=\"/pages/why-clean-seafood-matters\">How we source it</a>.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "q_frozen",
        "q_arrive",
        "q_store",
        "q_thaw",
        "q_whole",
        "q_feeds",
        "q_testing"
      ],
